<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  009 original four-wheel skid-steer AMR: PER ROBOT TEMPLATE.

  This file is a template, not a spawnable model. scripts/render_model.py replaces
  the __ROBOT__ token and writes a concrete model into runtime/models/amr_4wd_<robot>/.
  Templates exist because two robots sharing one SDF is how two robots end up
  driving each other: if the topics are relative, gz scopes them per model and the
  bridge has to guess the scoped name, and if the topics are shared, both robots
  listen to one command. Rendering one model per robot makes every topic and every
  frame name ABSOLUTE and therefore unambiguous.

  Absolute names this template produces for robot r01:
      /r01/cmd_vel             (in)     gz.msgs.Twist
      /r01/odom                (out)    gz.msgs.Odometry
      /r01/tf                  (out)    gz.msgs.Pose_V
      /r01/joint_states        (out)    gz.msgs.Model
      /r01/scan                (out)    gz.msgs.LaserScan
  There is no per-robot ground-truth topic; truth comes from the world level
  /world/<world>/dynamic_pose/info. See the note at the end of this file for the
  three attempts that established that, two of which failed silently.
  and TF frames r01/odom -> r01/base_footprint, so the prefix is applied exactly
  once and can never be applied twice by accident.

  Design notes that are usually got wrong:

  * The wheels are INSIDE the declared footprint. The footprint is 0.60 x 0.45 m
    and the wheel outer faces sit at y = +/-0.225, so nothing sticks out past the
    footprint the costmap and the evaluator use. If wheels overhung, the robot
    would clip obstacles its planner believed it had cleared.
  * Wheelbase 0.52 m, track 0.41 m, measured to the wheel CONTACT POINTS, which is
    what the differential-drive kinematics actually use.
  * NOT ackermann and NOT omnidirectional. Four fixed wheels, skid steer: the pair
    on each side rotates together. Calling this an ackermann base would make every
    downstream turn-radius assumption wrong.
  * Mass and inertia are plausible for a small AMR, NOT measured.
  * The lidar is gpu_lidar, because this gz-sim generation rejects the bare lidar
    type.
  * <timeout> on DiffDrive is the DOWNSTREAM protection that F13 requires: if the
    command stream stops arriving, gz itself zeroes the wheels. Without it, a
    client that dies mid command leaves the robot driving forever.

  Frame conventions match MASTER_PLAN section 8:
      <robot>/base_footprint -> <robot>/base_link -> <robot>/laser_link
-->
<sdf version="1.10">
  <model name="amr_4wd">

    <link name="base_footprint"/>

    <link name="base_link">
      <pose>0 0 0.175 0 0 0</pose>

      <inertial>
        <mass>22.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.70</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>1.10</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>1.55</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>

      <collision name="base_collision">
        <geometry>
          <box><size>0.60 0.45 0.20</size></box>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction><ode><mu>0.6</mu><mu2>0.6</mu2></ode></friction>
          <contact><ode><max_vel>0.01</max_vel><min_depth>0.001</min_depth></ode></contact>
        </surface>
      </collision>

      <visual name="base_visual">
        <geometry>
          <box><size>0.60 0.45 0.20</size></box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.20 0.24 0.30 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.26 0.31 0.38 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <visual name="deck_marker">
        <pose>0 0 0.102 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box><size>0.30 0.20 0.004</size></box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.10 0.55 0.85 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.15 0.65 0.95 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <visual name="mast_visual">
        <pose>0.20 0 0.13 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.025</radius><length>0.10</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.15 0.15 0.15 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <!-- ================= wheels ================= -->
    <!-- Contact points: x = +/-0.26, y = +/-0.205, radius 0.075 -->
    <link name="wheel_front_left">
      <pose>0.26 0.205 0.075 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>1.4</mass>
        <inertia><ixx>0.0025</ixx><iyy>0.0047</iyy><izz>0.0025</izz></inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="collision">
        <!-- rpy 90 deg about x points the cylinder's Z axis along y, i.e. it makes this
             a wheel. Without it the shape is a flat disc lying on the floor, and the
             robot drags rather than rolls: measured 0.043 m/s of a commanded 0.20 m/s,
             and 7.9 deg of a commanded 86 deg turn. -->
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.077</radius><length>0.040</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <surface>
          <!-- The collision radius is 2 mm LARGER than the 0.075 m visual radius, on
               purpose. With the collision exactly tangent to the floor the contact is a
               line with zero penetration, and measured behaviour was wheels spinning at
               their commanded rate while the robot advanced 0.02 m of a commanded 0.60 m
               and rotated 0.00 deg of a commanded 86 deg: no traction at all. The extra
               2 mm guarantees a real contact. It costs a 2.7% odometry scale error
               (rolling radius 0.077 vs the 0.075 the DiffDrive plugin integrates), which
               is recorded rather than hidden. -->
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <!-- mu2 far below mu, with fdir1 along the rolling direction, so the wheel
                   can scrub sideways. With isotropic friction (mu = mu2) the four fixed
                   skid-steer wheels refuse to scrub, and a commanded in-place turn
                   becomes forward sliding: measured left wheels 0.59 rad against right
                   wheels 2.902 rad, and no body yaw change at all.
                   See docs/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md. -->
              <mu>1.1</mu>
              <mu2>0.15</mu2>
              <fdir1>1 0 0</fdir1>
            </ode>
          </friction>
          <contact><ode><max_vel>0.1</max_vel><min_depth>0.002</min_depth></ode></contact>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.075</radius><length>0.040</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.08 0.08 0.08 1</ambient><diffuse>0.12 0.12 0.12 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <link name="wheel_rear_left">
      <pose>-0.26 0.205 0.075 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>1.4</mass>
        <inertia><ixx>0.0025</ixx><iyy>0.0047</iyy><izz>0.0025</izz></inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="collision">
        <!-- rpy 90 deg about x points the cylinder's Z axis along y, i.e. it makes this
             a wheel. Without it the shape is a flat disc lying on the floor, and the
             robot drags rather than rolls: measured 0.043 m/s of a commanded 0.20 m/s,
             and 7.9 deg of a commanded 86 deg turn. -->
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.077</radius><length>0.040</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <surface>
          <!-- The collision radius is 2 mm LARGER than the 0.075 m visual radius, on
               purpose. With the collision exactly tangent to the floor the contact is a
               line with zero penetration, and measured behaviour was wheels spinning at
               their commanded rate while the robot advanced 0.02 m of a commanded 0.60 m
               and rotated 0.00 deg of a commanded 86 deg: no traction at all. The extra
               2 mm guarantees a real contact. It costs a 2.7% odometry scale error
               (rolling radius 0.077 vs the 0.075 the DiffDrive plugin integrates), which
               is recorded rather than hidden. -->
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <!-- mu2 far below mu, with fdir1 along the rolling direction, so the wheel
                   can scrub sideways. With isotropic friction (mu = mu2) the four fixed
                   skid-steer wheels refuse to scrub, and a commanded in-place turn
                   becomes forward sliding: measured left wheels 0.59 rad against right
                   wheels 2.902 rad, and no body yaw change at all.
                   See docs/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md. -->
              <mu>1.1</mu>
              <mu2>0.15</mu2>
              <fdir1>1 0 0</fdir1>
            </ode>
          </friction>
          <contact><ode><max_vel>0.1</max_vel><min_depth>0.002</min_depth></ode></contact>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.075</radius><length>0.040</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.08 0.08 0.08 1</ambient><diffuse>0.12 0.12 0.12 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <link name="wheel_front_right">
      <pose>0.26 -0.205 0.075 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>1.4</mass>
        <inertia><ixx>0.0025</ixx><iyy>0.0047</iyy><izz>0.0025</izz></inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="collision">
        <!-- rpy 90 deg about x points the cylinder's Z axis along y, i.e. it makes this
             a wheel. Without it the shape is a flat disc lying on the floor, and the
             robot drags rather than rolls: measured 0.043 m/s of a commanded 0.20 m/s,
             and 7.9 deg of a commanded 86 deg turn. -->
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.077</radius><length>0.040</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <surface>
          <!-- The collision radius is 2 mm LARGER than the 0.075 m visual radius, on
               purpose. With the collision exactly tangent to the floor the contact is a
               line with zero penetration, and measured behaviour was wheels spinning at
               their commanded rate while the robot advanced 0.02 m of a commanded 0.60 m
               and rotated 0.00 deg of a commanded 86 deg: no traction at all. The extra
               2 mm guarantees a real contact. It costs a 2.7% odometry scale error
               (rolling radius 0.077 vs the 0.075 the DiffDrive plugin integrates), which
               is recorded rather than hidden. -->
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <!-- mu2 far below mu, with fdir1 along the rolling direction, so the wheel
                   can scrub sideways. With isotropic friction (mu = mu2) the four fixed
                   skid-steer wheels refuse to scrub, and a commanded in-place turn
                   becomes forward sliding: measured left wheels 0.59 rad against right
                   wheels 2.902 rad, and no body yaw change at all.
                   See docs/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md. -->
              <mu>1.1</mu>
              <mu2>0.15</mu2>
              <fdir1>1 0 0</fdir1>
            </ode>
          </friction>
          <contact><ode><max_vel>0.1</max_vel><min_depth>0.002</min_depth></ode></contact>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.075</radius><length>0.040</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.08 0.08 0.08 1</ambient><diffuse>0.12 0.12 0.12 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <link name="wheel_rear_right">
      <pose>-0.26 -0.205 0.075 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>1.4</mass>
        <inertia><ixx>0.0025</ixx><iyy>0.0047</iyy><izz>0.0025</izz></inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="collision">
        <!-- rpy 90 deg about x points the cylinder's Z axis along y, i.e. it makes this
             a wheel. Without it the shape is a flat disc lying on the floor, and the
             robot drags rather than rolls: measured 0.043 m/s of a commanded 0.20 m/s,
             and 7.9 deg of a commanded 86 deg turn. -->
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.077</radius><length>0.040</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <surface>
          <!-- The collision radius is 2 mm LARGER than the 0.075 m visual radius, on
               purpose. With the collision exactly tangent to the floor the contact is a
               line with zero penetration, and measured behaviour was wheels spinning at
               their commanded rate while the robot advanced 0.02 m of a commanded 0.60 m
               and rotated 0.00 deg of a commanded 86 deg: no traction at all. The extra
               2 mm guarantees a real contact. It costs a 2.7% odometry scale error
               (rolling radius 0.077 vs the 0.075 the DiffDrive plugin integrates), which
               is recorded rather than hidden. -->
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <!-- mu2 far below mu, with fdir1 along the rolling direction, so the wheel
                   can scrub sideways. With isotropic friction (mu = mu2) the four fixed
                   skid-steer wheels refuse to scrub, and a commanded in-place turn
                   becomes forward sliding: measured left wheels 0.59 rad against right
                   wheels 2.902 rad, and no body yaw change at all.
                   See docs/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md. -->
              <mu>1.1</mu>
              <mu2>0.15</mu2>
              <fdir1>1 0 0</fdir1>
            </ode>
          </friction>
          <contact><ode><max_vel>0.1</max_vel><min_depth>0.002</min_depth></ode></contact>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.075</radius><length>0.040</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.08 0.08 0.08 1</ambient><diffuse>0.12 0.12 0.12 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <link name="laser_link">
      <pose>0.20 0 0.30 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial><mass>0.12</mass><inertia><ixx>0.0001</ixx><iyy>0.0001</iyy><izz>0.0001</izz></inertia></inertial>
      <collision name="collision">
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.035</radius><length>0.045</length></cylinder></geometry>
      </collision>
      <visual name="visual">
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.035</radius><length>0.045</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.05 0.05 0.05 1</ambient><diffuse>0.1 0.1 0.1 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <sensor name="scan" type="gpu_lidar">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <topic>/__ROBOT__/scan</topic>
        <update_rate>10</update_rate>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <visualize>false</visualize>
        <lidar>
          <scan>
            <horizontal>
              <samples>360</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>-3.14159265</min_angle>
              <max_angle>3.14159265</max_angle>
            </horizontal>
          </scan>
          <range>
            <min>0.12</min>
            <max>12.0</max>
            <resolution>0.01</resolution>
          </range>
          <noise>
            <type>gaussian</type>
            <mean>0.0</mean>
            <stddev>0.01</stddev>
          </noise>
        </lidar>
      </sensor>
    </link>

    <!-- ================= joints ================= -->
    <joint name="base_footprint_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_footprint</parent>
      <child>base_link</child>
      <pose>0 0 -0.175 0 0 0</pose>
    </joint>

    <joint name="laser_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>laser_link</child>
    </joint>

    <joint name="wheel_front_left_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>wheel_front_left</child>
      <axis><xyz>0 1 0</xyz><limit><lower>-1e16</lower><upper>1e16</upper></limit></axis>
    </joint>
    <joint name="wheel_rear_left_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>wheel_rear_left</child>
      <axis><xyz>0 1 0</xyz><limit><lower>-1e16</lower><upper>1e16</upper></limit></axis>
    </joint>
    <joint name="wheel_front_right_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>wheel_front_right</child>
      <axis><xyz>0 1 0</xyz><limit><lower>-1e16</lower><upper>1e16</upper></limit></axis>
    </joint>
    <joint name="wheel_rear_right_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>wheel_rear_right</child>
      <axis><xyz>0 1 0</xyz><limit><lower>-1e16</lower><upper>1e16</upper></limit></axis>
    </joint>

    <!-- ================= plugins ================= -->

    <!-- Skid steer: the two wheels on each side are commanded together. Every
         topic and both TF frame names carry __ROBOT__, so two rendered models
         cannot collide on a topic or fight over a frame. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-diff-drive-system" name="gz::sim::systems::DiffDrive">
      <left_joint>wheel_front_left_joint</left_joint>
      <left_joint>wheel_rear_left_joint</left_joint>
      <right_joint>wheel_front_right_joint</right_joint>
      <right_joint>wheel_rear_right_joint</right_joint>
      <wheel_separation>0.41</wheel_separation>
      <!-- 0.077, the COLLISION radius, not the 0.075 visual one, and the difference is
           measured rather than argued.

           The wheel's collision cylinder was enlarged by 2 mm on purpose (see the note on
           the wheel links): with it exactly tangent to the floor no contact was generated
           and the robot had zero traction. But the wheel then rolls on a 0.077 m circle
           while this parameter said 0.075, so a commanded speed produced 2.7% more travel
           than asked for and odometry under-reported by the same amount.

           Measured confirmation, straight run at a commanded 0.25 m/s:
               simulator truth travelled  3.568 m
               odometry reported          3.475 m
               ratio                      1.0268
               collision / visual radius  0.077 / 0.075 = 1.0267
           Agreement to 0.04%. So this is a real systematic scale error, not noise, and it
           belongs here: this parameter is the radius the controller integrates with, so it
           must equal the radius the wheel actually rolls on.

           Note the stop distances and arrival errors measured before this change are 2.7%
           optimistic and were re-measured after it. -->
      <wheel_radius>0.077</wheel_radius>
      <max_linear_acceleration>0.5</max_linear_acceleration>
      <min_linear_acceleration>-0.5</min_linear_acceleration>
      <max_angular_acceleration>1.0</max_angular_acceleration>
      <min_angular_acceleration>-1.0</min_angular_acceleration>
      <max_linear_velocity>0.35</max_linear_velocity>
      <min_linear_velocity>-0.35</min_linear_velocity>
      <max_angular_velocity>0.6</max_angular_velocity>
      <min_angular_velocity>-0.6</min_angular_velocity>
      <odom_publish_frequency>20</odom_publish_frequency>
      <!-- Downstream protection (F13): with no command for this long, gz zeroes
           the wheels by itself. A client dying mid command must not leave the
           robot driving. -->
      <timeout>0.5</timeout>
      <topic>/__ROBOT__/cmd_vel</topic>
      <odom_topic>/__ROBOT__/odom</odom_topic>
      <tf_topic>/__ROBOT__/tf</tf_topic>
      <frame_id>__ROBOT__/odom</frame_id>
      <child_frame_id>__ROBOT__/base_footprint</child_frame_id>
    </plugin>

    <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-state-publisher-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::JointStatePublisher">
      <topic>/__ROBOT__/joint_states</topic>
      <joint_name>wheel_front_left_joint</joint_name>
      <joint_name>wheel_rear_left_joint</joint_name>
      <joint_name>wheel_front_right_joint</joint_name>
      <joint_name>wheel_rear_right_joint</joint_name>
    </plugin>

    <!-- ======================= ground truth =======================
         There is NO ground-truth plugin here. This is deliberate, and it is the third
         attempt at this channel, so all three are recorded.

         1. PosePublisher with <publish_link_pose>true</publish_link_pose> on
            /__ROBOT__/ground_truth/pose, bridged as gz.msgs.Pose_V.
            `gz topic -i` showed gz publishing gz.msgs.Pose while the bridge subscribed as
            gz.msgs.Pose_V. Nothing matched, the ROS topic existed with a publisher, no
            message was ever delivered, and no error was logged anywhere. A verification
            channel that is silently empty is worse than none, because it looks exactly
            like a robot that never moves.

         2. The same plugin with <publish_model_pose>true</publish_model_pose> and
            <publish_link_pose>false</publish_link_pose>, bridged as gz.msgs.Pose ->
            geometry_msgs/msg/Pose. `gz topic -i` then reported "No publishers on topic":
            that option combination emits nothing at all.

         3. The world-level /world/<world>/dynamic_pose/info, bridged as
            gz.msgs.Pose_V -> tf2_msgs/msg/TFMessage. This one DOES deliver: eight
            transforms per message, the first of which is the model pose at the correct
            coordinates. But that mapping drops the pose NAME and leaves the stamp at
            zero, so what arrives is a list of unnamed, unstamped points and the model
            has to be picked out by array position. That is good enough for a coarse
            single-robot cross-check and NOT good enough to be the verification channel
            the plan requires.

         Attempt 3 is the one left in place, and it is wired up in the launch file. The
         open work is explicit: a name-bearing truth channel needs either a small node on
         gz-transport or the ros_gz_interfaces route, neither of which is a bridge rule.
         Recorded as NOT_RUN rather than declared working.

         When it exists, this channel is for INITIALISATION, FAULT INJECTION and
         VERIFICATION only. Control must never subscribe to it: using ground truth as
         position feedback would make every accuracy claim circular (AGENTS.md rule 13). -->

  </model>
</sdf>
